  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nRadar imagery at 12 PM was showing scattered showers moving from \nnorth to south mainly across coastal areas. Over the past 6 hours, \nplaces north of San Diego County have received mainly less than \n0.05\", with totals ranging from 0.05 to 0.30\" in San Diego County.\nAdditional showers will continue at times through the day, most \nlikely in San Diego County, eventually tapering off overnight. \n\nBy tonight, models are showing a closed upper level low moving south \nand east towards the border of California and Arizona. This will \nturn the pressure gradient weakly offshore, increasing north to \nnortheasterly winds in wind prone locations. Peak wind gusts will be \nin the 30 to 45 mph range, strongest on the foothills of the San \nBernardino mountains and just below the Cajon Pass. Given the \nunsettled weather quickly turning to offshore flow, marine layer low \nclouds and fog will largely be absent from the coast into early next \nweek. \n\nHigh temperatures over the weekend will warm. Highs Saturday are \nexpected to be 5 to 10 degrees warmer than today, with additional \nwarming along the coast on Sunday and a few degrees of cooling or no \nchange further inland. By Monday, high temperatures in coastal areas \nwill be 5 to 10 degrees above average, with temperatures closer to \naverage further inland. Cooler conditions expected for the middle to \nend of next week.\n\nGlobal ensembles are indicating the potential for unsettled weather \nfor the end of next week. There remains a lot of spread in solutions \nand uncertainty in the details. Rain chances are in the forecast as \nearly as Friday. NBM chances for 0.01 inch or more of rainfall for \nthe coast to the mountains increases from 10 to 20 percent for next \nFriday to 35 to 45 percent on Saturday the 31st to 40 to 50 percent \non Sunday November 1st. In addition to precipitation, cooler, \ncloudier, and windier conditions may develop.",
